From: =?UTF-8?q?Lajosh=C3=A1zi=2C=20L=C3=A1szl=C3=B3=20Gergely?=
 <lajoshazilg@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 3 Aug 2026 11:46:20 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] camera: keep a resolution change from looking like a broken
 camera

Every resolution change tears the pipeline down and builds it again, and a
pipeline error during that was reported to the user as "Could not play camera
stream" over a camera that was about to come back on its own. Only the size
probe was recognised as deliberate; a size chosen in the preferences, and the
switch to the capture resolution and back that every still photograph makes,
were not. Those are the two places it was reported from.

An error while a switch is in flight now returns to the last size that was seen
to deliver frames, or looks for one if nothing has yet, and says so in the log
instead of in the user's face. The switch back after a capture is verified the
same way a chosen size is, rather than assumed to have worked.

The idle inhibitor is part of the same failure. It was released whenever the
viewfinder left Ready - which it does on every resolution change - so the idle
timer got a window each time the camera did anything. Measured on a Fairphone 3:
the screen blanked mid-reconfiguration while the viewfinder was open, the
software ISP's GPU debayer went on drawing into buffers that were no longer
mapped, and some 28000 unhandled IOMMU faults later the camera was dead until
the application restarted. Loading now keeps the inhibitor; only NoCameras and
Error release it.

Also adds what the size question was missing on both sides: a photo resolution
of its own, rather than always the sensor's largest, and a JPEG quality with an
estimate of what a picture will cost at it. The estimate is labelled as one -
what a JPEG actually costs depends on the picture, and no formula can be right.

And a flash photograph focuses again once the light is on. The focus the
viewfinder was holding was reached in the dark, which is the situation the flash
exists to fix; focusing before the light measures the wrong scene.
